  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para><emphasis>Three Different Forms of Linear Equations</emphasis></para>
<para>Work with a partner to answer 1 - 3.</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the slope-intercept form of a linear equation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y = mx + b\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the point-slope form of a linear equation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y -y_1= m (x -x_1 )\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the standard form of a linear equation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(Ax + By = C\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<para><emphasis>Using the Different Forms to Write Equivalent Equations</emphasis></para>
<para>Linear equations can be written in the different forms to create linear equations. Use the linear equation forms from 1 - 3 to help you complete the rest of the activity. Remember to show all of your work on your paper and to use inverse operations to move terms to different sides of the equation.</para>
<para>For 4 - 5, use the equation \(y-4=3(x+1)\).</para>

<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y=3x+7\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write the equation in standard form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(3x-y = -7\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write \(4x -6y =12\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y=\frac{4}{6}x-2\) orb \(y=\frac{2}{3}x-2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>

<note class="self-check">
  <para>Which of the following is equivalent to \(y=4x +5\) in standard form?</para>
</note>

<section><title>Additional Resources</title>
<section><title>Writing Equivalent Linear Forms</title>
  <para>Write an equation of a line in all three forms that has a slope of 2 and a \(y\)-intercept of 7.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Slope-Intercept Form</emphasis>
    Slope-intercept form is \(y=mx + b\) where m is the slope and \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept.
    Substitute \(m=2\) and \(b=7\) to have the equation \(y = 2x + 7\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Standard Form</emphasis>
    Write the same equation in standard form.
    Standard form is \(Ax + By =C\).</para>
  <para>Remember that \(A\) can not be negative and there should not be any fractions or decimals.</para>
  <para>For this form, rearrange slope-intercept form to lead with the \(x\) term:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Bring the \(x\) and \(y\) terms on the left side.
    \(y-2x = 2x-2x + 7\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Simplify.
    \(-2x + y = 7\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Divide all terms by -1 so that \(A\) is not negative.
    \(\frac{-2x}{ -1}+ \frac{y}{-1} = {7}{-1}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.
    \(2x - y = -7\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Point-Slope Form</emphasis>
    Point-Slope form is \(y-y_1=m(x-x_1)\).
    Substitute the slope in for \(m \) and the point in for \((x_1, y_1)\).
    \(m= 2\), \(x_1=0\), \(y_1=7\)
    \(y-7=2(x-0)\)</para>
  <para>Notice this can easily be written in slope-intercept form by solving for \(y\):</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}y-7&amp;=&amp;2(x-0)\\y-7&amp;=&amp;2x-0\\y-7+7&amp;=&amp;2x-0+7\\y&amp;=&amp;2x+7\end{array}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Standard Form to Slope-Intercept Form</emphasis>
    Standard form can also be rearranged into slope-intercept form.</para>
  <para>Write \(3x - 5y = 14\) in slope-intercept form.
    Work to solve for \(y\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Bring the \(x\) term to the right side with the constant.
    \(3x -3x - 5y = 14 -3x\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Simplify.
    \(- 5y = 14 -3x\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Divide by the coefficient of \(y\). 
    \(\frac{-5y}{-5}=\frac{14}{5}-\frac{3}{5}x\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify and write in the correct order.
    \(
    y=-\frac{3}{5}x+\frac{14}{5}\)</para>
  
  
  <note class="try">
  <title>Try It: Writing Equivalent Linear Forms</title>
  
  <!--Q#-->
  <exercise>
    <problem>
      <para>Write the equation \(3x +8y =24\) in slope-intercept form.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
      
      
    
    <para>Compare your answer: \(y=-\frac{3x}{8}+3\)</para>
      
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3x +8y &amp;=&amp;24-3x \\8y &amp;=&amp;24-3x\\\frac{8y}{8} &amp;=&amp;\frac{24}{8}-\frac{3x}{8}\\y&amp;=&amp;-\frac{3x}{8}+3\end{array}\)</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
  <!--Interaction End --></note></section>
  </section>
</content>
